description:
Found by the s394 review probe of s393d/#314 F-A1 (the "new axes are filed" half of the s369 filler rule).  NOT introduced by F-A1: the scalar spelling had it since the lead-decl branch shipped; F-A1 extends the same lowering to containers.

REPRODUCERS (perl oracle first, PCL second):

    $x = 9; my $x, $b1 = $x + 1; print "$main::b1\n";
    # perl: 10    PCL: 1

    @x = (1,2); my @x, @y = @x; print scalar(@main::y), "\n";
    # perl: 2     PCL: 0

CAUSE: _lower_block lead-decl branch (Pl/Parser2.pm, _lead_decl_with_expr_tail shape) lowers the whole `VAR <trailing>` tail INSIDE the fresh let.  Perl rule: a `my` variable is not visible until the statement AFTER the declaration, so every read of VAR in the tail sees the OLD binding (package global or outer lexical).

FIX SKETCH: evaluate the tail with the old binding still in scope â e.g. lower the tail void-expression BEFORE the let.  The lead VAR operand itself is the fresh lvalue in perl, but in this shape it is a bare VAR in void context, so its value is unobservable without overload; the observable part is reads of VAR in the REST of the tail.  Probe the `my $a . $foo` non-comma shape too.

RARITY: needs non-strict code whose tail side-effect READS the declared name â occurs nowhere in the corpus or the companion suite (opbasic/cmp.t is 12078/12078 without it).  Low priority.

BAR: both reproducers + the non-comma shape vs perl; corpus-diff; gate; guard rows (my-decl-tail-01.t is 14 s â check wall time before adding, else start -02).
